 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Organisation",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "1 Kbit of EEPROM arranged as a single block of 128 bytes, addressed 00h to 7Fh, on a two-wire I²C-compatible bus. The device is always a slave. Writes go through an 8-byte page buffer. Inputs have Schmitt triggers, and the output edge rate is controlled to limit ground bounce."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Variants",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "24AA01 runs from 1.7 V to 5.5 V in the industrial range, limited to 100 kHz below 2.5 V. 24LC01B runs from 2.5 V to 5.5 V at up to 400 kHz in the industrial (-40 °C to +85 °C) and automotive (-40 °C to +125 °C) ranges. A part number with T after the device name is supplied on tape and reel; SOT-23 and SC-70 come only that way."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bus conditions",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "The bus is idle when SDA and SCL are both high, and a transfer may begin only then. SDA may change only while SCL is low; a falling SDA edge with SCL high is a Start, a rising SDA edge with SCL high is a Stop. Every command begins with a Start and finishes with a Stop. Each bit takes one clock. The receiver of each byte acknowledges it by holding SDA low through a ninth clock supplied by the master. When reading, the master leaves the last byte unacknowledged so the device releases SDA and the master can issue the Stop."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Addressing",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "After a Start the first byte is the control byte: control code 1010, three block-select bits that this part ignores, and R/W (1 to read, 0 to write). On seeing 1010 the device acknowledges. A word address byte follows for writes and random reads; its seven low bits select the byte."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Byte write",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Start, control byte having R/W = 0, word address, one data byte, each acknowledged by the device, then Stop. The Stop starts the self-timed programming cycle (TWC, 5 ms max), during which the device acknowledges nothing."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Page write",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "As a byte write, but the master sends up to 8 data bytes before the Stop. They wait in the on-chip buffer and are programmed together after the Stop. After each byte the low address bits step up by one while the upper bits stay fixed, so more than 8 bytes, or a run that crosses a page boundary (a multiple of 8), wraps to the start of the same page and overwrites what was loaded there. Software has to keep each page write inside one page."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Write protect",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "WP tied to VCC blocks writes to the whole array (00h to 7Fh); tied to VSS, writes are allowed. Reads are unaffected either way."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Acknowledge polling",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Since the device does not acknowledge while a write cycle runs, the master can find the end of the cycle without waiting the full TWC: right after the Stop it sends a Start and a write control byte (R/W = 0). No acknowledge means still busy, so it repeats; an acknowledge means the cycle is over and the next command can follow at once."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current address read",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "An internal address counter points one past the byte most recently accessed. A Start followed by a control byte having R/W = 1 returns the byte at that address; the master does not acknowledge it and sends a Stop."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Random read",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "To read a chosen address, the master starts a write: control byte having R/W = 0 and the word address, which loads the address counter. It then sends a new Start (no Stop) and the control byte having R/W = 1; the device returns the byte, which the master leaves unacknowledged before its Stop."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sequential read",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Begun like a current-address or random read; the master acknowledges each byte instead of stopping, and the device sends the byte at the next address each time. The counter steps after every byte, so the whole array can be read in one transfer."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Noise protection",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "A VCC detector blocks internal erasing and writing while VCC is under about 1.5 V. SCL and SDA have Schmitt triggers and filters that reject spikes (TSP, 50 ns max)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reliability",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Endurance above 1 million erase/write cycles, data retention beyond 200 years, ESD above 4000 V on all pins. Factory programming is offered. All packages are lead-free and meet RoHS."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package notes",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "On every leaded outline, the body length D and width E1 exclude mold flash, which may add up to 0.010 in per side on PDIP, 0.15 mm per side on SOIC, TSSOP and MSOP, and 0.127 mm per side on SOT-23 and SC-70. The pin 1 mark may take various forms within a marked corner zone. DFN and TDFN are saw singulated and may show exposed tie bars at the ends; the TDFN contacts may have either of two end shapes. Dimensions follow ASME Y14.5M; BSC is a basic, exact value and REF a reference value."
  }
 ],
